From: Photo Wall <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: backend-drm: use 64-bit plane masks

The DRM backend gives every KMS plane an index (drm_plane::plane_idx, in
the zpos order the plane list is sorted into) and records the planes a
buffer or a view may use as a mask with one bit per index:
drm_fb::plane_mask and the candidate mask in
drm_output_find_plane_for_view. Both masks are 32 bits wide and built with
`1 << plane_idx`, so on a device with more than 32 planes the shift
overflows (undefined behaviour; in practice index N lands on bit N % 32)
and two planes share one bit.

The Raspberry Pi 5's vc4 exposes 56 planes. Sorted by zpos, the overlays
take indices 0-15, the writeback-only overlays 16-47, the cursors 48-51
and the primaries 52-55, so the first HDMI output's primary plane (index
54) shares bit 22 with an overlay. drm_output_find_plane_for_view visits
that overlay first, clears the shared bit and never offers the primary:
a client buffer is never scanned out on it, and every frame is composited
by the renderer.

Make every plane mask 64 bits wide and build each bit with one helper,
drm_plane_mask_bit(), so no plane bit is built with a 32-bit shift again.
A plane whose index would not fit in 64 bits is refused where the indices
are handed out (create_sprites logs and destroys it, and a virtual
output's plane is not created) instead of sharing a bit; the helper
asserts the index fits. Each plane's KMS id, type and index is logged once
at startup, so the mapping can be read from the log.

This supersedes !1902 ("backend-drm: support 64-bit plane_mask"), which
widened state-propose.c but left drm_fb::plane_mask 32 bits wide in fb.c
and put no bound on the index.

Bug: https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wayland/weston/-/issues/1078
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-17
---
--- a/libweston/backend-drm/drm-internal.h
+++ b/libweston/backend-drm/drm-internal.h
@@ -296,7 +296,8 @@ struct drm_fb {
 	int width, height;
 	int fd;
 
-	uint32_t plane_mask;
+	/* One bit per drm_plane::plane_idx: drm_plane_mask_bit() */
+	uint64_t plane_mask;
 
 	/* Used by gbm fbs */
 	struct gbm_bo *bo;
@@ -434,6 +435,20 @@ struct drm_plane {
 	struct weston_drm_format_array formats;
 };
 
+/* A plane mask (drm_fb::plane_mask and the candidates of
+ * drm_output_find_plane_for_view) holds one bit per plane index. Drivers
+ * expose more than 32 planes (vc4 on the Raspberry Pi 5 has 56), so the mask
+ * is 64 bits wide, and a plane whose index would not fit is refused when the
+ * planes are indexed rather than given an aliased bit. */
+#define DRM_PLANE_MASK_BITS 64
+
+static inline uint64_t
+drm_plane_mask_bit(const struct drm_plane *plane)
+{
+	assert(plane->plane_idx < DRM_PLANE_MASK_BITS);
+	return UINT64_C(1) << plane->plane_idx;
+}
+
 struct drm_connector {
 	struct drm_device *device;
 
--- a/libweston/backend-drm/drm-virtual.c
+++ b/libweston/backend-drm/drm-virtual.c
@@ -140,9 +140,14 @@ drm_virtual_plane_create(struct drm_device *device, st
 	if (weston_drm_format_add_modifier(fmt, mod) < 0)
 		goto err;
 
-	weston_plane_init(&plane->base, b->compositor);
-
 	plane->plane_idx = get_drm_plane_index_maximum(device) + 1;
+	if (plane->plane_idx >= DRM_PLANE_MASK_BITS) {
+		weston_log("%s: no plane index left: a plane mask holds only "
+			   "%d planes\n", __func__, DRM_PLANE_MASK_BITS);
+		goto err;
+	}
+
+	weston_plane_init(&plane->base, b->compositor);
 	wl_list_insert(&device->plane_list, &plane->link);
 
 	return plane;
